  we need to be holding this terrible Tory  government to account and that's what  I'd like to be doing and that's what I'm  sure all my colleagues would like to be  doing right hold on to account then  about rail fares I mean in all honesty  they they didn't go as much as they have  up as much as they have done in the past  well of course it's welcome that they're  limiting rail fares film fare rises this  year but unfortunately many commuters  won't feel very satisfied by that when  they've seen their fares on average go  up by 25 percent and some commuters as a  result of ministers decisions are seeing  their rail fares rise by almost 40%  since David Cameron came to power in  2010 so a paint a picture of the  railways under labour you would  eventually nationalize all the the  operators and indeed the rail network  you therefore control fares and make  sure they don't go up at all or are cut  well we want to make sure that  passengers and taxpayers get a better  deal under this Tory government we've  seen fares rocketing we've seen delays  and problems to investment and we know  from the government's own McNulty report  that we've got our fragmented system is  up to forty percent less efficient than  the best railways in Europe so we think  was an opportunity to do better by  tackling that fragmentation by bringing  franchises back into public ownership  and that can be used to fund investment  or to limit fare rises in the future  what about hs2 I listen to your  conference speech last year and you  mentioned there yes we need the  high-speed rail and you talked about  connecting the Midlands and the north I  mean am I wrong here could I read into  that that you think pats hs2 could be  built the other way round than the  current plans well I'm a great supporter  of hs2 and it's incredibly important you  know I'm standing here in Nottingham's  you're travelling from Nottingham to  Birmingham is a very slow journey as it  is from Nottingham to Leeds those  journeys could be transformed by hs2  it's time and we want the government to  get on and get it built the whole of the  y network yet but it is that I mean  which part of the Y the prongs or the  straight bit because the current plan is  London to Birmingham first perhaps the  other bits first well obviously well I  mean we're a long way  on with the London to Birmingham part of  hs2 that legislation is well on its  route through Parliament that was the  government's decision to split it into  two I think that's regrettable we could  have got on with the whole system but we  are where we are it's important that  that part of the legislation continues  its passage through Parliament and gets  that the construction gets underway and  that we get on with phase two it's very  disappointing that the government  haven't yet confirmed the route and  station stops for Phase two and  potentially won't until the end of this  year we think they should provide some  certainty and then local authorities and  cities and people up and down the route  can get on with planning so that we  maximise the benefits of this investment  in expanding our rail network okay and  does your disappointment with the  government extend to the issue of  another runway at heathrow of course the  Davis Commission said right let's get on  with it that's a that's the one where  plumping for we were told that a  definitive decision would be taken  before Christmas of course it wasn't  would you like to see them going for  that I think it's pretty disgraceful  that the Prime Minister promised the  decision by the end of last year and  then didn't manage to deliver one reason  wasn't anywhere around to even defend  his decision it's clear there are real  issues to be tackled particularly around  how any a new runway in the southeast  could be compatible with you air quality  directive particularly if they wanted to  make that new runway at heathrow but we  knew all of that infant that when Davis  reported back in the summer and it's  shameful that the government have  actually just delayed the decision fort  seems like political reasons to avoid a  by-election in Richmond Park is that  gold to me when to step down as he's  threatened to do okay so just to be  clear labour supports right now a third  runway at heathrow  labor support a new runway in the  southeast we think that's necessary  we'll be able to take a decision purple  on government on whatever the government  proposes when we see it well we need we  need further information from the  government you just mentioned Davis  Heathrow are on the table well you must  have read the Davis condition you said  he went along with Davis and Davis says  Heathrow we welcome Davis's a report we  think that's a really helpful  contribution but there are some real  questions over the local impact both in  terms of noise and in terms of air  quality and until we see proper  proposals from the government including  more environmental information we're not  able to to make a firm decision at one  way or the other it's for the government  to bring those forward and it's very  shameful that they have booted that down  the road for clearly political reasons  when they could have got on with that  sooner that sounds a bit like  government's position to me this is one  last question that's all a couple of  cyclists go beside you there we of  course saw your leaders Christmas card  we know about his love of bikes some  this government seems to be cutting the  cycling infrastructure budget would you  restore those cuts well that we're a  long way off the next general election  and and the sort of ending commitments  we want to make but i think it is  disappointing that the government talked  about a cycling revolution and then went  on to make really large cuts to the  cycling budget in the comprehensive  spending review we want to see more  people cycling and we want to see a  long-term commitment to investment in  cycling and walking all right good to  talk to you thank you very much indeed  shadow transport secretary lily and  Greenwood there

